/* Complete English companion for the concept cards in living-concepts.js.
 * Keep keys stable: callers select cards by concept id. These are game explanations,
 * not legal, clinical, or financial advice. */
const EX_CONCEPTS_EN = {
  perma:['PERMA · five dimensions of well-being','Seligman’s model separates positive emotion, engagement, relationships, meaning and accomplishment. A Sim can feel joy and still feel lonely.','Documented experiences affect the five game values. They are not a clinical measure or a judgment of anyone’s worth.','https://ppc.sas.upenn.edu/node/708','Which area needs attention even though other areas are already supported?'],
  P:['P · Positive emotion','Joy, gratitude and calm are examples of positive emotions. Difficult experiences can temporarily lower this dimension.','A financial buffer can support a sense of security; money does not automatically create happiness.','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3122271/','What small good moment could the Sim notice without dismissing a real concern?'],
  E:['E · Engagement','Engagement grows when a task holds attention and fits a person’s abilities and interests. A good challenge is neither constant overload nor simply waiting.','Actual learning, craft and focused activity can strengthen E.','', 'Does the challenge fit the Sim’s ability, and is there a visible next step?'],
  R:['R · Relationships','Reliable, voluntary closeness and mutual support contribute to well-being. The number of contacts alone says little about their quality.','Experienced care, friendship and wanted support affect R.','','Which relationship helps this Sim feel seen and supported?'],
  M:['M · Meaning','Meaning comes from experiencing one’s actions as significant and connected to something beyond an immediate reward. That may be family, community, creativity or a personal commitment.','Real help and personally important activities can strengthen M. A higher bank balance alone cannot.','','Who or what does the Sim want to make a difference for?'],
  A:['A · Accomplishment','Accomplishment concerns experienced progress and mastering personal tasks. Success need not be prestigious or publicly visible.','Recorded learning and completed plans affect A; a setback does not erase earlier progress.','','What small step deserves recognition, even without applause?'],
  self_efficacy:['Self-efficacy','The belief that one’s actions can meet a particular challenge. This expectation is distinct from the skill a person actually has.','Successful attempts, setbacks and support change a Sim’s expectations. Self-efficacy is situation-specific.','https://dictionary.apa.org/self-efficacy','What concrete experience could show the Sim that their actions can make a difference?'],
  gross:['Gross pay','Agreed wages before income tax and employee contributions. Employer contributions are recorded separately in the game.','Gross pay is not all available to spend. The simulation uses fictional, simplified deductions.','','Which part of the agreed pay goes toward which form of support?'],
  net:['Net pay','Pay after the deductions included in the calculation. Expected net pay is a forecast; available net pay requires an actual payment.','Monthly statements count money received. Taxes and contributions are not deducted a second time from net pay.','','Has the income arrived, or is it only expected later?'],
  cold_rent:['Base rent','Rent for the home before operating and heating costs. Electricity and other contracts may cost extra.','Listings show base rent and deposit. Check the budget for additional housing costs.','https://www.bpb.de/kurz-knapp/zahlen-und-fakten/sozialbericht-2024/553255/mieten-und-wohnkosten/','How do additional costs change the apparent price of this home?'],
  warm_rent:['Rent and utilities','Operating and heating costs are added to base rent. A rent including utilities does not automatically cover every private contract, such as household electricity.','The game combines utilities and heating in a simplified budget; it is not a real utility bill.','','Which private contracts are still missing from the housing budget?'],
  health_insurance:['Health coverage','Health insurance pools resources to support access to health services. Contributions and covered services follow their own rules.','In the game, health funds are accounted for separately from town funds. Fictional contributions do not represent real German rates.','https://www.bundesgesundheitsministerium.de/gkv/seite'],
  social_insurance:['Social contributions','Social insurance covers different risks, such as illness, care, retirement and unemployment. Contributions are not the same as income tax.','The simplified payslip separates employee contributions, employer contributions and tax. Not every contribution goes to town hall.'],
  reserve:['Emergency reserve','Savings can provide room for repairs, necessary purchases or lost income. A useful amount depends on real commitments.','Monthly reserve planning reduces the amount available for other plans. It neither withdraws money nor duplicates it in another account. The game’s target is two months of expenses, with a minimum of 500 currency units.','','Which unexpected expense could seriously disrupt the current budget?'],
  projection:['Forecast · not a payment','A forecast estimates income and costs using current agreements. Work completed, available employer funds and unexpected events can change the result.','Next month’s estimate combines accessible funds and expected income, then subtracts costs, outstanding bills and the planned reserve. Other private accounts remain private.','','Which assumption would change the result most if it proved wrong?'],
  liquidity:['Liquidity','Money currently accessible for payments. Property value, expected wages and a locked deposit are not immediately available funds.','The combined view includes personal and shared accounts. Transfers between them do not create income.','','Is the Sim wealthy, able to pay today, or both—and what would need to be sold?'],
  deposit:['Rental deposit','Security for a rental agreement. It remains tied up; it is neither consumed food nor freely available savings.','The deposit is recorded separately and returned through a documented transaction when the agreement ends properly. Declining an application does not trigger payment.'],
  credit:['Credit · payment and interest','A loan provides money while also creating an obligation to repay it. A payment may include principal and interest.','Loan proceeds are not earned income. Repayments appear in the cost plan; financing requires sufficient funds and acceptance.','','What flexibility does the loan create today, and what fixed obligations follow?'],
  arrears:['Outstanding bills','Unpaid bills that have already come due differ from costs that are only planned. A shortfall may call for clarification, help or a new agreement.','The forecast accounts for existing bills in addition to upcoming monthly costs.','','Which costs have already arisen and must not disappear into future planning?'],
  care:['Care and self-directed support','Support needs vary. Being older does not automatically make someone incapable or remove their right to choose. Family care requires agreement, time and respite.','Some Sims receive care at home; others live at Lindenblick Senior Residence. Relatives make actual visits, while professional services are explicitly paid external services.','','What support does the person want, and who gives the caregiver relief?'],
  care_insurance:['Care coverage and out-of-pocket costs','Care coverage pays part of agreed care services. Other costs may remain the person’s responsibility.','Game contributions and support levels are fictional, not official care classifications. Any out-of-pocket share appears as its own bill.','https://www.bundesgesundheitsministerium.de/themen/pflege/online-ratgeber-pflege/die-pflegeversicherung'],
  education:['Education and credentials','An education record names an institution, period, subject, qualification and, where applicable, a result. Enrollment does not mean a qualification has been earned.','Starting biographies are marked as procedurally supplemented. New qualifications require documented learning and attendance; a biography does not grant a professional license.','','Which record proves a qualification, and what practical ability must also be shown?'],
  grade:['Educational results · context matters','A grade summarizes performance on particular assessments; it is not a measure of someone’s worth or complete capability. Initial qualifications are fictional biographical records, while observed learning has its own dated evidence.\n\nBennington uses an explicitly labeled approximate 0–4 GPA scale, where higher is stronger. Legacy towns retain their original lower-is-stronger scale. Neither grading convention is a direct probability of success at work.','Required credentials and practiced skills are assessed separately in job applications.','','What kind of task did this result measure—and what did it not measure?'],
  application:['Applications and selection','A suitable qualification creates an opportunity, not a guaranteed contract. An application can be declined even when stated requirements are met.','Jobs consider skill, relevant education and documented experience with that employer. Housing decisions consider disclosed financing, deposit and documented reliability. A new application is allowed after seven days; a decline changes neither contract nor balance.'],
  probability:['Probability','An estimate describes a chance under current assumptions. It does not prove intent or guarantee an outcome.','Applications use a reproducible W100 roll, so repeated clicks cannot reroll the same request.','','What assumptions underlie this estimate, and how could new information change it?'],
  tom:['Theory of mind · social perspective','People try to infer what others want, know and intend. Such guesses can be mistaken and shaped by personal experience and current needs.','The Sim distinguishes observation, possible interpretation, personal wishes and open questions. Suspected interest is not consent.','','What did the Sim observe, what are they inferring, and what could they ask?'],
  emotion:['Feelings and intensity','Feelings respond to needs, expectations and experienced situations. Several feelings can occur at once and fade over time.','The percentage shows intensity in the game, not probability or a psychological diagnosis. Sources and duration are recorded in the personal log.'],
  need:['Needs · urgency','A high value here means a stronger unmet need, not better care. Needs can influence feelings and choices.','Eating, sleeping and wanted social contact reduce the relevant urgency only when the actions actually happen.'],
  romantic_affection:['Romantic affection','Wanting romantic closeness is different from social warmth and from consenting to a particular action.','Under 14, the value is always zero. Ages 14–17 are capped at 0.35 and limited to age-appropriate, nonsexual teen romance with at most a one-year age gap. Sexual interactions involving minors are excluded. Adults may have higher values; consent remains a separate requirement.','','Is the closeness wanted by both people, age-appropriate and free from pressure?'],
  social_warmth:['Social warmth','The wish for reliable contact, belonging and friendly closeness can exist at any age. It does not imply romance or sexuality.','Family contact, friendship, desired help and conversation can support social warmth.','','Does the Sim need listening, shared activity, reliable help or simply company?'],
  skill:['Skill','Practiced ability in a particular area. A skill differs from personality, wealth and a formal qualification.','Actual practice changes skills. Job requirements and W100 attempts take them into account.','','What specific activity was practiced, and how can the ability be observed?'],
  attribute:['Personal attribute','A relatively stable capacity, such as attention or stamina. It describes a strength in one area, not a Sim’s overall worth.','Attributes and practiced skills work together; needs and strain affect current performance.','','What conditions help this capacity, and what might be obscuring it now?'],
  goal:['Ambition and progress','An ambition describes what matters to a Sim over the longer term. Progress comes from relevant experiences.','A goal may concern money, learning, relationships, creativity or stability. Meeting one need does not automatically fulfill every goal.','','Is the Sim pursuing a personal goal or a standard set by someone else?'],
  reputation:['Reputation · known evidence','Reputation grows from visible actions and other people’s experiences. Possessions, helpfulness and reliability are separate dimensions.','Employers consider documented experience. Unverified rumors and private thoughts are not automatically treated as wrongdoing or lack of skill.','','Who knows what from which experience—and what is still only rumor?'],
  w100:['W100 · one uncertain attempt','A roll from 1 to 100 is compared with a target based on skill, attributes and situational strain.','A successful roll means the action went well. It never substitutes for another Sim’s consent.'],
  status:['Current work, education and care','This status distinguishes employment, school, study, job seeking, retirement and agreed care. It is separate from the room a Sim is currently in.','A Sim can be employed while at home. A care break and family care are tracked separately from a new employment contract.','','Which roles provide structure, and which obligations compete for time?'],
  amusement:['Amusement','Amusement can follow something the Sim finds playful, funny or entertaining. What counts as amusing depends on the person and the moment.','Read this changing game intensity with the event that prompted it; it is not a diagnosis.'],
  elation:['Elation','Elation is a strong uplift that may follow unusually welcome news or a meaningful success. The same event can affect different Sims differently.','Read this changing game intensity with the event that prompted it; it is not a diagnosis.'],
  pleasure_ecstasy:['Pleasure / ecstasy','This label represents especially intense pleasure in the game. Its source and meaning depend on the recorded experience; the label alone does not imply intimacy or consent.','Read this changing game intensity with the event that prompted it; it is not a diagnosis.'],
  contentment:['Contentment','Contentment can accompany a sense that things are going well enough for now. It may come from ordinary security or a satisfying moment, not only a major achievement.','Read this changing game intensity with the event that prompted it; it is not a diagnosis.'],
  thankfulness_gratitude:['Thankfulness / gratitude','Gratitude may arise when the Sim notices help, care or something they value. It does not create a debt or obligation to the person who helped.','Read this changing game intensity with the event that prompted it; it is not a diagnosis.'],
  affection:['Affection','Experienced friendly or family closeness can prompt affection. It is not automatically romantic or sexual.','Intensity is a changing game value, not a diagnosis. The Sim’s own event history provides important context.'],
  infatuation:['Infatuation','Infatuation can involve strong attention to or excitement about someone. It describes the Sim’s own state, not the other person’s feelings or agreement.','Read this changing game intensity with the event that prompted it; it is not a diagnosis.'],
  hope_enthusiasm_optimism:['Hope / enthusiasm / optimism','Hope, enthusiasm and optimism describe possible emotional states. Their causes depend on the situation, needs and personal interpretation.','Intensity is a changing game value, not a diagnosis. The Sim’s own event history provides important context.'],
  triumph:['Triumph','Triumph can follow overcoming a challenge or reaching an important goal. It may be experienced privately and need not involve defeating another person.','Read this changing game intensity with the event that prompted it; it is not a diagnosis.'],
  pride:['Pride','A successful personal step can prompt pride. Success can be acknowledged without putting others down.','Intensity is a changing game value, not a diagnosis. The Sim’s own event history provides important context.'],
  interest:['Interest','Interest draws attention toward a person, idea or activity that seems relevant or engaging. It can prompt curiosity without guaranteeing follow-through.','Read this changing game intensity with the event that prompted it; it is not a diagnosis.'],
  awe:['Awe','Awe may arise when something feels strikingly vast, beautiful or beyond the Sim’s usual expectations. Its meaning depends on what they experienced.','Read this changing game intensity with the event that prompted it; it is not a diagnosis.'],
  astonishment_surprise:['Astonishment / surprise','Astonishment and surprise describe possible emotional states. Their causes depend on the situation, needs and personal interpretation.','Intensity is a changing game value, not a diagnosis. The Sim’s own event history provides important context.'],
  concentration:['Concentration','Concentration means attention is focused on a current task or thought. Fatigue, interruptions and competing needs can make it harder to sustain.','Read this changing game intensity with the event that prompted it; it is not a diagnosis.'],
  contemplation:['Reflection','Reflection is a pause to consider an experience, choice or possibility. It does not mean the Sim has reached a conclusion.','Read this changing game intensity with the event that prompted it; it is not a diagnosis.'],
  relief:['Relief','Relief can arise when pressure eases or support arrives. It is distinct from long-term financial security.','Intensity is a changing game value, not a diagnosis. The Sim’s own event history provides important context.'],
  longing:['Longing','Longing describes a possible emotional state shaped by a wish, memory or unmet need. Its meaning depends on the Sim’s own experience.','Intensity is a changing game value, not a diagnosis. Interpret it alongside the Sim’s recorded experiences.'],
  teasing:['Teasing','Teasing can be playful when it is welcome, but the same words can feel hurtful in another relationship or situation.','Interpret the event using the people’s relationship, boundaries and reported experience; intent alone does not determine impact.'],
  impatience_and_irritability:['Impatience and irritability','Irritability may reflect frustration, fatigue, unmet needs or a difficult interaction. It does not by itself reveal hostility or intent.','Read intensity alongside the Sim’s needs and recent events; the interpretation may change with new information.'],
  sexual_lust:['Sexual desire','Desire is a private state and never establishes another person’s interest or consent. The simulation excludes sexual interactions involving minors.','This value is separate from affection, relationship status and consent.'],
  doubt:['Doubt','Doubt can signal uncertainty about an interpretation, plan or relationship. It is not proof that something is wrong.','Consider what the Sim knows, what they are guessing and what information is still missing.'],
  fear:['Fear','Fear can respond to perceived danger or uncertainty. Its intensity does not establish that a threat is objectively present.','Use the recorded situation and the Sim’s perspective as context; support and new information may change the response.'],
  distress:['Distress','Distress can arise when demands exceed available resources or support. It is an experience, not a character flaw.','Intensity can change as circumstances and needs change. The event history gives more context than the score alone.'],
  confusion:['Confusion','Confusion can mean that information is incomplete, conflicting or difficult to interpret.','A clarifying question or new evidence may help; avoid treating uncertainty as deception.'],
  embarrassment:['Embarrassment','Embarrassment can follow unwanted attention or a perceived social mistake. The Sim’s interpretation may differ from what others noticed.','Read the event and relationship context before inferring how others judged the Sim.'],
  shame:['Shame','Shame can make a person feel exposed or unworthy. A painful feeling does not establish that the person deserves blame.','Separate the Sim’s feeling from the facts of the event and the possibility of repair.'],
  disappointment:['Disappointment','Disappointment can follow an unmet expectation. It may point to something important without proving that the goal is impossible.','Compare what the Sim expected with what happened and what remains within their control.'],
  sadness:['Sadness','Sadness may follow loss, distance or unmet hopes. It can coexist with care, meaning and moments of joy.','The intensity can change over time; consider the Sim’s own memories and circumstances.'],
  bitterness:['Bitterness','Bitterness may reflect a lasting sense of unfairness or hurt. It is a clue to an experience, not proof that every interpretation is accurate.','Look for the underlying need or unresolved event without dismissing the feeling.'],
  contempt:['Contempt','Contempt is a strong negative judgment of another person. A momentary value does not define the whole relationship or either person.','Consider the event, boundaries and history; do not treat a score as a final verdict.'],
  disgust:['Disgust','Disgust can be a response to an experience, object or perceived boundary violation. Its cause is personal and contextual.','Use the event record to understand what prompted the response rather than assuming a fixed trait.'],
  anger:['Anger','Anger can signal frustration, threatened boundaries or perceived unfairness. It does not by itself justify harmful action.','A pause, clear boundary or practical change may help; intensity is not permission to harm.'],
  malevolence_malice:['Malice','A high negative intensity does not establish a plan or prove intent. Interpret actions from evidence rather than inferring them from a feeling score.','Keep feelings, intentions and completed actions distinct.'],
  sourness:['Resentment','Resentment can build when a grievance feels unresolved. It may point toward a boundary or conversation that needs attention.','Identify the specific experience and consider whether a repair or changed agreement is possible.'],
  pain:['Pain','Pain is a difficult experience that can affect attention, energy and social responses. It should not be read as poor motivation.','Consider practical care and rest; the game value is not a medical diagnosis.'],
  helplessness:['Helplessness','Helplessness can arise when influence feels absent or hard to see. A small real area of choice may differ from a large unreachable solution; accepting help can itself be an action.','A job seeker cannot control the market, but can prepare a suitable application or ask someone for information.','','What can the Sim influence, and where is help needed rather than more willpower?'],
  fatigue_exhaustion:['Fatigue and exhaustion','Exhaustion concerns available energy, not moral reliability. A full calendar cannot be extended by motivation alone; rest, fewer tasks and real relief are distinct options.','If a relative is caring for a parent and sleeping little, praise may help, but it does not replace someone taking over a visit.','','Which task can actually be dropped or taken over by someone else?'],
  emotional_numbness:['Emotional numbness','Muted experience can make a person’s situation harder to read. Little visible emotion does not mean relationships are unimportant; memories and wishes provide more context.','A Sim may respond quietly to a visit and later remember it as reliable contact. The outward response alone was incomplete.','','What meaning appears in the record even if the moment showed little expression?'],
  intoxication_altered_states_of_consciousness:['Intoxication and altered states','Altered perception can make judgments and boundaries less reliable. The game distinguishes a documented adult experience from invented intoxication, and never treats it as consent.','After a documented adult experience, a Sim may misjudge a task. Others can arrange a safe pause instead of adding pressure.','','Is the situation safe, and which decision should wait under these conditions?'],
  jealousy_envy:['Jealousy and envy','Envy often compares one’s opportunities with another person’s; jealousy more often concerns threatened closeness. Either can reveal a wish, but neither proves infidelity or unfairness.','A Sim who sees their partner talking with a friend can ask for closeness without assuming a secret intention.','','Am I missing something of my own, or did I observe a specific broken agreement?'],
  hunger:['Hunger · physical urgency','In the game, lack of food increases physical urgency and can strain a Sim’s patience. Other Sims usually see only the reaction; they do not automatically know its cause.','An actual meal reduces hunger. Income and supplies shape which meals are accessible.','','What physical strain might be shaping this social interpretation?'],
  thirst:['Thirst','Even an easily met need can demand attention when distance, cost or an ongoing task delays care. Physical urgency and personal intent are separate.','Actually drinking reduces urgency; merely saying it is planned does not perform the action.','','What small act of care could make the next step easier?'],
  bladder:['Bladder / need for a restroom','Everyday planning includes ordinary but time-sensitive needs. A Sim who cuts a conversation short may have a practical reason rather than rejecting the other person.','The simulation looks for an accessible suitable room. Completing the restroom action reduces the value.','','What other explanation could there be for the abrupt goodbye?'],
  fatigue:['Tiredness','Rest helps make energy available. When work, care and leisure compete for the same hours, motivation alone cannot create more sleep.','Actual rest and sleep reduce urgency. Needs and strain affect current performance.','','Which commitment could be renegotiated so rest can really happen?'],
  fun:['Variety and enjoyment','Leisure can provide self-directed room. An enjoyable activity need not be expensive; what fits depends on the Sim’s interests and accessible options.','Suitable activities reduce urgency when they actually happen. Spending money is only one possible route.','','Which free activity fits this Sim’s interests?'],
  hygiene:['Hygiene','Physical comfort and feeling ready to be present with others can intersect. A missed routine may reflect lack of resources or time, not lack of care.','The need falls after the hygiene activity is actually completed in a suitable place. The value is not a moral judgment.','','What condition is making care harder right now?'],
  comfort:['Comfort','A welcoming place to rest creates space between demands. Comfort, luxury and security are different: a simple home can still be reliable and restorative.','Actual suitable rest affects the game value; visible possessions alone do not meet the need.','','What makes this place restful for the Sim?'],
  reasoning:['Reasoning','Thinking through a problem does not mean its solution has already been practiced.','This attribute works with practiced skill and situational strain in relevant W100 attempts. It is a game model, not a judgment of the whole person.','','What skill and conditions would help this capacity become useful?'],
  coordination:['Coordination','Coordinating an action well differs from already knowing the right technique.','This attribute works with practiced skill and situational strain in relevant W100 attempts. It is a game model, not a judgment of the whole person.','','What skill and conditions would help this capacity become useful?'],
  presence:['Presence','Appearing confident and persuasive is not the same as being right or deserving trust.','This attribute works with practiced skill and situational strain in relevant W100 attempts. It is a game model, not a judgment of the whole person.','','What skill and conditions would help this capacity become useful?'],
  resolve:['Persistence','Staying with a goal can help; changing course can be just as reasonable as persisting.','This attribute works with practiced skill and situational strain in relevant W100 attempts. It is a game model, not a judgment of the whole person.','','What skill and conditions would help this capacity become useful?'],
  perception:['Perception','Noticing a detail and understanding its meaning are two different steps.','This attribute works with practiced skill and situational strain in relevant W100 attempts. It is a game model, not a judgment of the whole person.','','What skill and conditions would help this capacity become useful?'],
  stamina:['Stamina','Stamina is a capacity; available energy also depends on rest and strain.','This attribute works with practiced skill and situational strain in relevant W100 attempts. It is a game model, not a judgment of the whole person.','','What skill and conditions would help this capacity become useful?'],
  strength:['Strength','Physical strength can make some tasks easier, but does not replace technique or support.','This attribute works with practiced skill and situational strain in relevant W100 attempts. It is a game model, not a judgment of the whole person.','','What skill and conditions would help this capacity become useful?'],
  life_progression:['A life unfolds through transitions','A birthday changes age, not achievement. A young adult may still be completing school; graduation needs recorded attendance and learning. Retirement ends the regular contract while preserving earned wages. These are simplified game rules, not local legal eligibility.\n\nA partnership, a household agreement and a housing contract are three separate commitments. Mutual interest can lead to dating; sustained conflict may lead to separation. Moving needs an affordable home and an accepted application, and neither a breakup nor a new partner silently takes someone else’s money.','Turbo runs these same rules for every minute. Recent major changes appear here; their events and subjective experiences remain in the personal journal.'],
  local_economy:['Bennington · the town and its game economy','The setting uses Bennington streets and selected public institutions, with fictional residents and private businesses. New towns use US dollars and approximate local age-cohort proportions; individual households are invented.\n\nTo preserve the existing game mechanics, taxes, social contributions, rent, care coverage and wage levels use the configurable Vivarium policy. They are a simplified social economy, not a simulation of Vermont law, actual benefit eligibility or real employer salaries.','Income, expense, reserve and class calculations use this town’s currency consistently. Open the household forecast to inspect the assumptions rather than infer real-world entitlements.'],
  shared_goals:['Shared goals · cooperation needs an opportunity','In the Robbers Cave study, working toward goals that required cooperation helped reduce friction between rival groups. Simply putting people in the same place was not equivalent to giving them something meaningful to accomplish together. This was a specific camp study with boys, not a universal rule for every friendship or marriage.\n\nThe design implication is to create occasions for mutual contribution: a work problem, a garden, a family responsibility or learning something together. People can then notice reliability, ask for help and acknowledge effort. Partners can also maintain a good relationship while enjoying different hobbies.','Known shared interests and responsibilities influence contact choices. Accepted invitations create actual destinations and tasks. Cooperative encounters add experience to relationships and meaning; merely displaying a shared-goal label grants no reward.','https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Sherif/chap7.htm'],
  response_style:['How we respond when something goes well','Gable and colleagues studied sharing positive events and the responses that follow. Perceived enthusiastic, constructive responses were associated with benefits for positive emotion and relationship wellbeing. An interested follow-up can help someone revisit what the event means, rather than merely hearing a quick “good for you.”\n\nThe four styles distinguish active engagement from a brief or absent response, and constructive acknowledgment from deflation or dismissal. These describe responses, not permanent kinds of people. A tired friend may respond poorly without intending harm; the recipient’s experience and the responder’s intention can differ.','Personality, trust, tension, fatigue and communication practice influence four probabilities. The resulting response is logged and affects both relationship and wellbeing. It remains a game heuristic rather than a calibrated prediction.','https://www.sas.rochester.edu/psy/people/faculty/reis_harry/assets/pdf/GableReisImpettAsher_2004.pdf'],
  big_five:['Personality · tendencies can change slowly','The Big Five describe broad tendencies rather than fixed identities. Extraversion concerns social approach and stimulation; conscientiousness concerns organization and persistence; openness concerns exploration; agreeableness concerns cooperation; emotional sensitivity concerns responsiveness to threat and strain. There is no single ideal combination for every situation.\n\nA randomized study of a three-month digital intervention found changes in targeted personality traits among people motivated to change. That supports taking sustained practice seriously, but not claiming that one reassuring conversation transforms a personality. Introversion is not an illness, and support should respect the person’s own aims.','Traits influence action preferences, task planning and social initiative. Communication practice changes response tendencies gradually. A difficult day changes state and behavior much faster than it changes a durable trait.','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33558417/'],
  ambition:['Ambition · effort, direction and methods','A strong wish to achieve something can support persistence, but it does not specify the methods a person will choose. Two ambitious colleagues may collaborate, compete fairly, seek recognition or undermine one another depending on trust, resources and their willingness to cooperate.\n\nDistinguish the importance of a goal from confidence that it is achievable. Small documented successes can support self-efficacy; repeated blocked opportunities may call for a different plan or practical help rather than simply more determination.','Ambition and cooperative tendency influence social motives. Shared work can produce collaboration or rivalry; skills, opportunities and actual action still determine progress.'],
  income_class:['Household resources · compare like with like','The same income supports different living standards depending on household size. This view divides expected household net income by the square root of the number of members, a transparent game equivalence rule that recognizes some shared costs. It then compares the result with the reference shown in the calculation.\n\nIncome, wealth and liquidity answer different questions. A homeowner may have valuable property but too little cash for a repair. A high income may coexist with large fixed commitments. Class bands here are descriptive game bands, not official classifications or predictions of someone’s character.','The displayed calculation uses expected income; actual paid transactions remain in the monthly statement. Reserves and forecast shortfalls drive the financial appraisal. Initial capital and new loans are not earned income.'],
  plan_activity:['Planning an activity · an intention with prerequisites','Planning creates a future intention. The Sim must reach the location, find the required time and resources and actually complete the activity before its benefits count. A work obligation, urgent need or accepted encounter may delay it.\n\nThe displayed activity price is checked by the relevant action when it is performed. Planning itself does not guarantee another Sim will participate; invitations and applications have their own acceptance rules.','Follow task starts, pauses, resumptions and completions in the personal journal. The world clock advances only when you request a step.'],
  support_action:['Booking support · what happens next','A support request creates a plan with a destination and appointment time. The Sim still needs to travel there and receive the service; pressing the button is not itself a completed counseling session. The personal log distinguishes the accepted plan from actual attendance.\n\nPractical support and communication practice can improve the options available to a Sim. Neither guarantees a particular emotion, erases a recorded experience nor makes another person agree. Financial and family constraints may still require separate action.','The support plan schedules care through the existing service operators. Inspect its appointment, cost and completed event in the personal records.'],
  application_action:['Applying · a possible match, not a guaranteed offer','An application is assessed against actual requirements and circumstances. Skills, necessary qualifications, affordability and documented prior interactions can change the result. Wealth cannot substitute for a required professional credential.\n\nAn unsuccessful application is evidence about this application at this time, not proof of personal worth. Another vacancy, training, a stronger budget or a later opportunity may change the options.','The application is recorded and can be accepted or declined. Only an accepted contract changes the job or home; viewing a listing does not reserve it.']
};
// Keep the richer authored research-card versions exactly aligned with the UI cards.
Object.assign(EX_CONCEPTS_EN, {
  life_progression:['A life unfolds through transitions','A birthday changes age, not achievement. A young adult may still be completing school; graduation needs recorded attendance and learning. Retirement ends the regular contract while preserving earned wages. These are simplified game rules, not local legal eligibility.\n\nA partnership, a household agreement and a housing contract are three separate commitments. Mutual interest can lead to dating; sustained conflict may lead to separation. Moving needs an affordable home and an accepted application, and neither a breakup nor a new partner silently takes someone else’s money.','Turbo runs these same rules for every minute. Recent major changes appear here; their events and subjective experiences remain in the personal journal.'],
  grade:['Educational results · context matters','A grade summarizes performance on particular assessments; it is not a measure of someone’s worth or complete capability. Initial qualifications are fictional biographical records, while observed learning has its own dated evidence.\n\nBennington uses an explicitly labeled approximate 0–4 GPA scale, where higher is stronger. Legacy towns retain their original lower-is-stronger scale. Neither grading convention is a direct probability of success at work.','Required credentials and practiced skills are assessed separately in job applications.'],
  local_economy:['Bennington · the town and its game economy','The setting uses Bennington streets and selected public institutions, with fictional residents and private businesses. New towns use US dollars and approximate local age-cohort proportions; individual households are invented.\n\nTo preserve the existing game mechanics, taxes, social contributions, rent, care coverage and wage levels use the configurable Vivarium policy. They are a simplified social economy, not a simulation of Vermont law, actual benefit eligibility or real employer salaries.','Income, expense, reserve and class calculations use this town’s currency consistently. Open the household forecast to inspect the assumptions rather than infer real-world entitlements.'],
  perma:['PERMA · several routes to wellbeing','Seligman separates positive emotion, engagement, relationships, meaning and accomplishment because a good life cannot be reduced to feeling cheerful. Deeply demanding work can provide meaning while feeling unpleasant; a pleasant afternoon can still leave someone lonely. Looking at the five dimensions separately makes those differences visible.\n\nThe model is a framework for asking better questions, not a prescription that everyone should maximize every score. In Vivarium, a low value is a clue to inspect experiences and circumstances. It does not diagnose illness or prove that someone has made bad choices.','Documented experiences affect particular dimensions. Financial security can support positive emotion without manufacturing friendship or meaning. Hovering a score is less informative than opening its actual experience sources.','https://ppc.sas.upenn.edu/node/708'],
  P:['Positive emotion · why small good moments matter','Fredrickson’s broaden-and-build theory proposes that positive emotions can temporarily widen the actions and possibilities a person considers. Curiosity, play and friendly contact may then build skills or social resources that remain after the pleasant feeling has passed. The useful distinction is between the momentary feeling and what a person does with the opportunity.\n\nThis is not a demand to stay positive. Fear, sadness and anger can convey important information, and positive thinking does not remove poverty, exhaustion or danger. A supportive encounter may create room for exploration while the underlying practical problem still needs attention.','Good experiences support P. Shared activities can also create independent evidence for engagement and relationships; the simulation does not raise all PERMA dimensions just because a Sim smiles.','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3122271/'],
  shared_goals:['Shared goals · cooperation needs an opportunity','In the Robbers Cave study, working toward goals that required cooperation helped reduce friction between rival groups. Simply putting people in the same place was not equivalent to giving them something meaningful to accomplish together. This was a specific camp study with boys, not a universal rule for every friendship or marriage.\n\nThe design implication is to create occasions for mutual contribution: a work problem, a garden, a family responsibility or learning something together. People can then notice reliability, ask for help and acknowledge effort. Partners can also maintain a good relationship while enjoying different hobbies.','Known shared interests and responsibilities influence contact choices. Accepted invitations create actual destinations and tasks. Cooperative encounters add experience to relationships and meaning; merely displaying a shared-goal label grants no reward.','https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Sherif/chap7.htm'],
  response_style:['How we respond when something goes well','Gable and colleagues studied sharing positive events and the responses that follow. Perceived enthusiastic, constructive responses were associated with benefits for positive emotion and relationship wellbeing. An interested follow-up can help someone revisit what the event means, rather than merely hearing a quick “good for you.”\n\nThe four styles distinguish active engagement from a brief or absent response, and constructive acknowledgment from deflation or dismissal. These describe responses, not permanent kinds of people. A tired friend may respond poorly without intending harm; the recipient’s experience and the responder’s intention can differ.','Personality, trust, tension, fatigue and communication practice influence four probabilities. The resulting response is logged and affects both relationship and wellbeing. It remains a game heuristic rather than a calibrated prediction.','https://www.sas.rochester.edu/psy/people/faculty/reis_harry/assets/pdf/GableReisImpettAsher_2004.pdf'],
  big_five:['Personality · tendencies can change slowly','The Big Five describe broad tendencies rather than fixed identities. Extraversion concerns social approach and stimulation; conscientiousness concerns organization and persistence; openness concerns exploration; agreeableness concerns cooperation; emotional sensitivity concerns responsiveness to threat and strain. There is no single ideal combination for every situation.\n\nA randomized study of a three-month digital intervention found changes in targeted personality traits among people motivated to change. That supports taking sustained practice seriously, but not claiming that one reassuring conversation transforms a personality. Introversion is not an illness, and support should respect the person’s own aims.','Traits influence action preferences, task planning and social initiative. Communication practice changes response tendencies gradually. A difficult day changes state and behavior much faster than it changes a durable trait.','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33558417/'],
  ambition:['Ambition · effort, direction and methods','A strong wish to achieve something can support persistence, but it does not specify the methods a person will choose. Two ambitious colleagues may collaborate, compete fairly, seek recognition or undermine one another depending on trust, resources and their willingness to cooperate.\n\nDistinguish the importance of a goal from confidence that it is achievable. Small documented successes can support self-efficacy; repeated blocked opportunities may call for a different plan or practical help rather than simply more determination.','Ambition and cooperative tendency influence social motives. Shared work can produce collaboration or rivalry; skills, opportunities and actual action still determine progress.'],
  income_class:['Household resources · compare like with like','The same income supports different living standards depending on household size. This view divides expected household net income by the square root of the number of members, a transparent game equivalence rule that recognizes some shared costs. It then compares the result with the reference shown in the calculation.\n\nIncome, wealth and liquidity answer different questions. A homeowner may have valuable property but too little cash for a repair. A high income may coexist with large fixed commitments. Class bands here are descriptive game bands, not official classifications or predictions of someone’s character.','The displayed calculation uses expected income; actual paid transactions remain in the monthly statement. Reserves and forecast shortfalls drive the financial appraisal. Initial capital and new loans are not earned income.'],
  plan_activity:['Planning an activity · an intention with prerequisites','Planning creates a future intention. The Sim must reach the location, find the required time and resources and actually complete the activity before its benefits count. A work obligation, urgent need or accepted encounter may delay it.\n\nThe displayed activity price is checked by the relevant action when it is performed. Planning itself does not guarantee another Sim will participate; invitations and applications have their own acceptance rules.','Follow task starts, pauses, resumptions and completions in the personal journal. The world clock advances only when you request a step.'],
  support_action:['Booking support · what happens next','A support request creates a plan with a destination and appointment time. The Sim still needs to travel there and receive the service; pressing the button is not itself a completed counseling session. The personal log distinguishes the accepted plan from actual attendance.\n\nPractical support and communication practice can improve the options available to a Sim. Neither guarantees a particular emotion, erases a recorded experience nor makes another person agree. Financial and family constraints may still require separate action.','The support plan schedules care through the existing service operators. Inspect its appointment, cost and completed event in the personal records.'],
  application_action:['Applying · a possible match, not a guaranteed offer','An application is assessed against actual requirements and circumstances. Skills, necessary qualifications, affordability and documented prior interactions can change the result. Wealth cannot substitute for a required professional credential.\n\nAn unsuccessful application is evidence about this application at this time, not proof of personal worth. Another vacancy, training, a stronger budget or a later opportunity may change the options.','The application is recorded and can be accepted or declined. Only an accepted contract changes the job or home; viewing a listing does not reserve it.']
});
// Preserve the source cards' reflection and example fields in English as well.
const EX_CONCEPTS_EN_CONTEXT = {
  E:[null,'Does the challenge fit the Sim’s ability, and is there a visible next step?','A new piece is too difficult, while a simple scale feels boring. A suitably small passage offers both challenge and feedback.'],
  M:[null,'Who or what does the Sim want to make a difference for?','Someone regularly helps in the neighborhood despite having little money. The work can feel meaningful without wealth or fame.'],
  self_efficacy:[null,'What concrete experience could show the Sim that their actions can make a difference?','A Sim feels they have no chance before applying for a job. Preparing and completing a good interview gives them a different personal experience, even while the contract remains undecided.'],
  health_insurance:[null,'Which risks are shared rather than depending only on the current balance?'], social_insurance:[null,'Which protection matches each life risk, and which fund receives the contribution?'],
  reserve:[null,'Which unexpected expense could seriously disrupt the current budget?','Two households earn the same amount. The one with larger fixed commitments has less freedom after an unexpected repair.'],
  gross:['https://www.bundesfinanzministerium.de/Web/DE/Themen/Steuern/Steuerarten/Lohnsteuer/lohnsteuer.php'],
  liquidity:[null,'Is the Sim wealthy, able to pay today, or both—and what would need to be sold?','A landlord owns a valuable house but does not have enough cash for today’s repair. Wealth and immediate ability to pay can diverge.'],
  deposit:[null,'Which funds are available but initially tied up in starting a new life?'], care_insurance:[null,'Which costs are shared, and what out-of-pocket amount remains in the household budget?'], application:[null,'What already fits, what could improve, and which parts of selection remain outside the applicant’s control?'],
  tom:[null,'What did the Sim observe, what are they assuming, and what could they ask?','A colleague gives a short reply. The Sim suspects rejection, though time pressure is also possible. Asking a follow-up can separate observation from assumption.'],
  emotion:[null,'Which expectation and need connect this event to the feeling?'], need:[null,'Which unmet need is physical, which is social, and what action would actually help?'],
  attribute:[null,'What conditions help this capacity, and what might be obscuring it now?','An attentive, persistent Sim fails at a task while tired. The result may reflect current conditions rather than disprove their underlying ability.'],
  w100:[null,'Was the target difficult, was execution uncertain, or were the circumstances unfavorable?'], status:[null,'Which roles provide structure and belonging, and which obligations compete for time?','An unemployed father cares for children and supports neighbors. The missing employment contract does not describe all his work or contribution.'],
  amusement:['Do both people find it funny, or is one laughing at the other?','While washing dishes, a child makes a pun and the parent laughs, even though an unpaid bill remains stressful. The light moment does not settle the bill, but may make conversation easier.'],
  elation:['Which decision can wait until tomorrow while I enjoy today’s success?','After receiving an offer, a Sim wants to celebrate and spend freely. They can enjoy the good news while checking next month’s plan first.'],
  pleasure_ecstasy:['What makes this moment enjoyable, and what do I hope will last beyond it?','A lovely evening feels wonderful; the next morning, the two people may still need to clarify whether they want the same kind of relationship.'],
  contentment:['Which expectations are mine, and which have I taken on from others?','A Sim with a modest income enjoys a dependable home and still hopes to learn drawing again.'],
  thankfulness_gratitude:['What exactly am I grateful for, and what freedom does each person retain?','A neighbor brings food. The recipient can appreciate the gesture without agreeing to every later request.'],
  affection:['What kind of care does the other person actually want?','A Sim repairs their sister’s bicycle, but today she would have preferred someone to listen quietly. Both wanted closeness but chose different ways to show it.'],
  infatuation:['What observation suggests interest, and what might I be reading into it?','Two 16-year-olds of the same age enjoy a shared hobby. An invitation for a walk can explore interest without treating friendliness as agreement.'],
  hope_enthusiasm_optimism:['What remains within my control if the hoped-for outcome does not happen?','An applicant hopes for an offer and practices for the interview. At the same time, she looks for a second suitable position.'],
  triumph:['Am I celebrating an achievement or another person’s defeat?','A team wins a contest. One Sim thanks their helpers; another mocks the losers. The same success leaves different traces in relationships.'],
  pride:['Which step am I proud of, regardless of comparison?','After weeks of practice, a piece of music finally comes together. The Sim recognizes their persistence even though someone else already plays better.'],
  interest:['What would I like to understand without having to master it immediately?','A senior asks about a grandchild’s school project and rediscovers their own interest in astronomy.'],
  awe:['What do I see differently from a wider perspective?','Looking at the stars, a Sim reflects on everyday worries and the people they want to spend time with.'],
  astonishment_surprise:['Which expectation was unsettled, and what information is still missing?','A colleague is waiting at the door. The Sim expects criticism, but the colleague is actually asking for help with a task.'],
  concentration:['Would more effort help, or would better conditions help more?','A hungry student rereads the same line. After eating and breaking the work into a clear task, it is easier to focus again.'],
  contemplation:['What information could actually move this reflection forward?','A Sim rereads a short message and suspects rejection. A direct question may clarify more than another round of speculation.'],
  relief:['What is truly resolved, and what has only become less urgent?','After arranging an installment plan, a Sim sleeps more easily. They still need to budget for the agreed payment next month.'],
  longing:['What quality do I miss, beyond the particular person or situation?','A Sim misses an old school friend. They may also miss the easy shared play that could be found again with new friends.'],
  teasing:['Has the audience changed, or has someone’s boundary become clear?','Two friends joke about a mishap. When more classmates join, one friend goes quiet and wants to change the subject.'],
  impatience_and_irritability:['What strain am I already bringing into this encounter?','After little sleep, a long wait and hunger, a Sim snaps at a harmless question. The other person does not know this background.'],
  sexual_lust:['Can both people agree, decline or change their minds without pressure?','Two adult partners want different amounts of closeness. They talk about it without treating a refusal as a failure of duty.'],
  doubt:['Which checkable assumption am I uncertain about?','A Sim doubts their chosen training path. Talking with an apprentice may help more than asking whether they are talented enough in general.'],
  fear:['What has happened, what do I fear, and what action could create room to respond?','After a large bill, a tenant fears eviction. They first check the actual amount due and what support is available.'],
  distress:['What resource is actually missing, and who could provide targeted relief?','A family caregiver has enough income but almost no free time and no one to cover an evening. The main shortage is time.'],
  confusion:['Which two pieces of information do not yet fit together for me?','A manager praises the work and postpones the project. The Sim does not yet know whether the delay is about their work or the budget.'],
  embarrassment:['What did others actually observe, and what reaction am I only imagining?','A teenager stumbles while reading aloud and thinks everyone is laughing. A friend mainly noticed how much the task mattered to them.'],
  shame:['Can I describe what happened without judging my entire worth?','After a rejection, a Sim thinks, “I’m no good at anything.” More precisely: “I did not get this job today; my other abilities still exist.”'],
  disappointment:['Do I want to change the goal or first try another route?','A class is canceled. The Sim is disappointed but keeps looking for a way to pursue their creative ambition.'],
  sadness:['Does this person need comfort, attention or practical help right now?','An older Sim misses their late partner. During a visit, they would like to share a memory rather than immediately discuss a new hobby.'],
  bitterness:['What new observation could genuinely change my old expectation?','After several broken promises, a Sim expects nothing good from a new neighbor. A small promise kept reliably can offer a different experience.'],
  contempt:['Which specific action am I criticizing, rather than devaluing the whole person?','A Sim calls a colleague lazy. So far, all that is known is that a task was left undone; the reason is still missing.'],
  disgust:['Is my aversion about a situation, or am I unfairly directing it at a person?','A Sim feels uncomfortable in a dirty kitchen. They can arrange cleaning without treating the resident as inferior.'],
  anger:['Which boundary or agreement is involved, and what fair request could I make?','A parent is upset about dishes left out and reads them as disrespect. The child thought someone else was responsible today; clarifying who does what may solve more than making an accusation.'],
  malevolence_malice:['Which fact is established, and where am I treating a fear as if it already happened?','After an argument, a Sim imagines revenge. Until an action is taken, no resulting harm has been documented.'],
  sourness:['Which experienced situation best explains this momentary state?','After an unpleasant remark, a Sim remains briefly upset. A later, friendly clarification may change that immediate reaction.'],
  pain:['What has become harder to do, and what support is wanted?','A senior can enjoy a visit and still need a break. Needing care does not remove their choice about what help they want.'],
  helplessness:[null,null,'An unemployed Sim cannot control the job market, but can prepare a suitable application and ask someone they know for information.'],
  fatigue_exhaustion:[null,null,'A relative cares for their mother and gets little sleep. Kind praise is welcome, but it does not replace someone taking over a visit.'],
  emotional_numbness:[null,null,'A Sim reacts quietly to a visit but later remembers it as reliable contact. The outward response alone was incomplete.'],
  intoxication_altered_states_of_consciousness:[null,null,'After a documented adult experience involving consumption, a Sim misjudges a task. Others arrange a safe pause instead of adding pressure.'],
  jealousy_envy:[null,null,'A Sim sees their partner talking with a friend for a long time. They can ask for closeness without assuming the conversation concealed an intention.']
};
let EX_CONCEPTS_EN_RESTORED_FIELDS = 0;
for (const [key, fields] of Object.entries(EX_CONCEPTS_EN_CONTEXT)) {
  const card = EX_CONCEPTS_EN[key];
  for (let i = 0; i < fields.length; i++) {
    const index = fields.length === 2 && fields[0] !== null ? i + 4 : i + 3;
    if (fields[i] && !card[index]) { card[index] = fields[i]; EX_CONCEPTS_EN_RESTORED_FIELDS++; }
  }
}
for (const card of Object.values(EX_CONCEPTS_EN)) {
  if (card.length === 3) card.push(null);
  if (card[3] === '') card[3] = null;
}

Object.assign(EX_CONCEPTS_EN,{
 attractiveness:['Attractiveness · appearance and individual attraction','A first impression and a close relationship draw on different information. An appealing appearance can invite attention, while unreliable or dismissive behavior can undermine attraction as people get to know one another. A high appearance score therefore never guarantees a date or a rewarding relationship.\n\nThe appearance attribute belongs to the adult Sim; attraction belongs to an observer. Inspect that observer’s weights for appearance, warmth, reliability, competence, status and shared interests. Small gender-associated offsets are fictional game settings with broad individual variation, not a rule about every man or woman.','Appearance and known reputation have stronger influence on adult social-choice weights. A need or a high score does not grant consent; age and kinship restrictions apply first. Minors have no adult appearance/attractiveness score.'],
 popularity:['Popularity · quality within a known network','Being widely known, being liked and being trusted are different things. Someone may have a small but supportive circle, or many contacts with little closeness. A public accomplishment can increase recognition without making every personal relationship warmer.','Popularity is the proportion of incoming contacts with closeness ≥50, trust ≥45 and tension <50. Read it with the number of contacts: one out of one is very different from fifty out of one hundred. No contacts means unscored, not universally disliked.'],
 social_drives:['Social drives · motives rather than commands','The same wish for recognition can lead to showing good work, helping a group, making a new connection or undermining a rival. The route depends on ambition, cooperation, strain and the relationship. This lets similar goals produce very different social lives.','Connection combines social need and extraversion. Achievement combines ambition and unmet accomplishment. Recognition combines ambition, unmet relationship support and competitive tendency. They change the probabilities of eligible social choices; plans, commitments and consent still constrain action.']
});
for(const key of ['attractiveness','popularity','social_drives'])EX_CONCEPTS[key]=EX_CONCEPTS_EN[key];
